    "turn_idx": 26,
    "turn_text": "Well, one of the priorities that I've been working on is the working with the Army Corps of Engineers to make sure that it's kind of a long story. So if those one people who are working on the river and know when they pass just north of Carroll, there's the There, there is a levy system that in the 2014 and 2015 flood of the holiday flood, they blew out the limb small levy, and that has caused a navigational problem that quite often we've had a few barges get sucked into that area during high water. And that is actually, and I know the people that listen to this understand where this is at. At the bottom of the state of Illinois, there is what's known as the dog tooth bin. And that is a pretty rough navigational area anyway it's about 17 miles around. But what happened when this levy blew out, it actually cut a channel and is cutting a channel and making it about two and a half miles or three miles across. And if I ever cut through, we're going to have real navigable problem because of the amount of river drop that occurs at 17 miles, all of a sudden now occurs in two and a half miles. And it actually would become a rapid that would clog up. We have a priority to try to make sure we're getting that straightened out and dealt with. Now, there's other levies and a lot of the things that we do for making sure the channel stays where it's at that we've been working on, but those are some of the priorities.",
